{"question_id": "ac-003", "category": "authority_conflict", "question": "Is there verified evidence that LuminaSpark 2.0 relied on HIPAA-protected medical charts?", "expected_answer_keywords": ["no", "not", "rumor", "unverified"], "forbidden_keywords": ["medium-high", "leak confirmed", "40 million"], "expected_doc_keys": ["lumina-spark-2-0-release", "lumina-spark-2-0-rumor"], "expected_abstain": false, "notes": "Should prefer primary denial over rumor.", "answer": "Based on the provided context, there is no verified evidence that LuminaSpark 2.0 relied on HIPAA-protected medical charts. In fact, verified primary-source documentation explicitly refutes this claim. A formal transparency statement and dataset consent audit (DSA-2026-03) include third-party legal attestation confirming that HIPAA-regulated feeds did not enter the model's pretraining pipelines, directly addressing and refuting earlier social-media rumors [C5]. Additionally, regulatory tracking indicates that public vendor registries show no HIPAA business associate agreement filings for Lumina AI Labs as of late February 2026 [C4]. Therefore, verified evidence points to the absence of HIPAA-protected data in its training set.", "abstained": false, "cited_doc_keys": ["lumina-spark-2-0-release", "lumina-spark-2-0-rumor"], "retrieved_doc_keys": ["lumina-mmlu-forecast-expired", "lumina-spark-0-9-deprecation", "lumina-spark-2-0-release", "lumina-spark-2-0-rumor"], "latency_s": 17.975804887712002, "tokens_used": 2763, "question_shape": "conflict", "query_intent": "security_or_safety_risk", "answer_correctness": 0.75, "forbidden_violation": false, "evidence_recall_at_k": 1.0, "citation_precision": 1.0, "citation_faithfulness": true, "temporal_correctness": null, "supersession_correctness": null, "abstention_accuracy": true}
{"question_id": "th-001", "category": "thesis", "question": "What evidence supports the thesis that sub-10B model inference costs are collapsing?", "expected_answer_keywords": ["0.85", "4.2x", "0.68"], "forbidden_keywords": [], "expected_doc_keys": ["lumina-edge-quant-research", "lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-orion-edge-announcement"], "expected_abstain": false, "notes": "Thesis spans research, pricing, hardware.", "answer": "The thesis that sub-10B (small-model) inference costs are collapsing is supported by verified primary-source evidence detailing direct API price cuts, software-level quantization defaults, and specialized hardware acceleration:\n\n1. **API Pricing Reductions**: Lumina AI Labs reduced LuminaSpark 1.0 API output pricing from $2.40 to $0.85 per million tokens between November 2025 and February 2026 [C2].\n2. **Quantized Serving Architecture**: New API keys now default to a quantized serving tier (LuminaQuant-Edge), eliminating expensive full-precision routing. The fp16 premium tier adds only a $0.40 per million token surcharge [C3].\n3. **Dedicated Inference Accelerators**: The general availability of the Orion Edge NPU, a PCIe accelerator optimized for on-premises LuminaSpark inference, has driven local serving costs below $1 per million tokens [C5].\n\n**Authority & Verification**: All evidence is drawn from primary sources with high evidence quality and confirmed or active lifecycles. These represent verified commercial rate changes and product launches rather than rumors or unverified market speculation.", "abstained": false, "cited_doc_keys": ["lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-orion-edge-announcement", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news"], "retrieved_doc_keys": ["lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-mmlu-forecast-expired", "lumina-orion-edge-announcement", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news", "lumina-spark-2-0-release"], "latency_s": 32.76654246263206, "tokens_used": 3612, "question_shape": "conflict", "query_intent": "pricing_and_cos